A||N||9||turbo86se||speaking of Indy Fieros..||||6||
Z||000000||turbo86se||12-13-1999||10:46 PM||||I know of two 84 Indy's in the Myrtle Beach area, I've seen another in Loris, and I've heard of one with a V8 in Charleston. Let me tell you about the two in Myrtle Beach. The first one is near the Jet Port, has less than 30,000 miles, 4-spd, mint interior, great paint, with a bad engine. The owner wants to put a 3800 in it, but he's been saying that for years. It's still sitting there, under a huge tree. He won't sell it, but he'll accept an unreal amount for it. He is the original owner, and has the  certificate! The second one is sitting in a man's tennis court beside his house near CCU. It is in real bad shape. It's been rear-ended (repairable), cracks in roof top near sunroof, 4-spd, 24,000 miles, loaded. Interior isn't too bad. Engine is bad. Here's the real bad part. Before he moved it to his house, he stored it under a hotel near the ocean for several years. Because of this, the bottom of the doors are badly rusted, and the part where the fascia is bolted to the frame is nearly gone. The front end is kinda hanging there. It would take some serious TLC, but this one could be nice again. Why do they have to neglect a Fiero? Especially when there's only 2000 of these beautiful cars made!||||reg||6
Z||000001||ChadMan||12-14-1999||08:46 AM||||I never see Indy Fieros anymore, except at shows.  The last time I saw one on the road was about three years ago, when I saw one dead at the side of the road.  The next day, I drove up to leave a note offering $500 for the car, but it was gone!<P>My best buddy's stepmom had an Indy Fiero (bought new in 1984), but totaled it shortly after buying it.  That leaves 1,999...||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||12-14-1999||11:10 AM||||IMHO I don't think that anyone should modify the rarer Fieros.  The Indy should remain and Indy, the blue '87s should stay blue, etc.  There are plenty of other, less desireable Fieros out there to modify.  Don't get me wrong, I'm all for modification.  And how did that guy get rust on the bottom of his door?  These have plastic door panels.||||reg||1
Z||000003||turbo86se||12-14-1999||11:26 AM||||Monkeyman, the exterior door panels were fine, but when I opened the door, there was rust deposits and rusty metal pieces everywhere. I ran my hand under the door, it was rough, and I had rust on my hand. It was probably the metal internals. The guy only wants $1000 for the car! I agree with you, I wouldn't modify an Indy either!||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||12-14-1999||02:07 PM||||turbo86se, You didn't really say if you are thinking about buying. But if you are... Be careful!!! The rust you can see is probably only a small percentage of what is really there. I wouldn't be surprised to find the whole unibody underneath the skin is rotted away (not to mention floor pans, trunk, every bolt/nut, etc...). That car is probably only good for parts.<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000005||turbo86se||12-14-1999||05:53 PM||||DRH, yeah I'm sure you're right about all the rust. For a while I really wanted to buy it, I offered him $800 cash early this summer, but he wanted $1000 firm. I haven't been back since, but I drive by every once in a while, it's still there. Isn't Fierodave looking for parts for a Indy? I'll have to email him! ||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||12-14-1999||08:23 PM||||before deciding to restore a car in a rough shape as that, get it on a lift and make sure the bottom is in good shape. If it's not then you're looking at moving the indy to a new frame.<P>don't worry about the doors. take the skins off and replace the door hardware. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||12-14-1999||09:06 PM||||Guys! Maybe not everyone feels this way, but that's an Indy Fiero and it sure deserves to be restored. Sure, it'll be expensive to repair all the frame rust, but it's an Indy. Geez! It's better that a Fiero nut buys it and fixes it up, then for it to end up as a parts car. I'll wager that Indy cars will increase in value over the years better than any other Fiero. That's my opinion.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||12-14-1999||09:34 PM||||I'm not surprised that the frame, etc is rusty being in all of that salt air.  If the frame is too bad to restore the way it is and you have the money to do it, I don't see why you couldn't strip the frame and put everything on a new frame.  It would take alot of work and big bucks, but I think we all agree that the Indy needs to stay an Indy.  Good Luck! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||superXmotorsports||12-14-1999||10:37 PM||||Ron Smigla Jr in this group owns and is restoring 2 Indy cars.  If anyone has any specific questions I would post a note to him or email him.  SXM.||||reg||3
